    "q-125981": {
      "dom_id": "step-q125981",
      "name": "Question 16",
      "problem": "If $f(x)=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡({x}^{2})$  and $x\\ge 0,$ calculate  ${f}^{-1}(x)$ from first-principles.",
      "worked_solution": "Let $y=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡{x}^{2}.$ Note that $f(x)$ is a one-to-one function on $x\\ge 0.$ We have $y\\in [0,1)$ for $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ \n\nTo find the inverse function we swap $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡{y}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in [0,1)$ and $y\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\nNext, we apply the definition of $\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{e}^{2{y}^{2}}-1}{{e}^{2{y}^{2}}+1} \\\\ x({e}^{2{y}^{2}}+1) & ={e}^{2{y}^{2}}-1 \\\\ (x-1){e}^{2{y}^{2}} & +(x+1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the ${e}^{2{y}^{2}}$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2{y}^{2}} & =-\\frac{x+1}{x-1} \\\\ {e}^{2{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{1+x}{1-x} \\\\ 2{y}^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}) \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y\\ge 0,$ we select the positive root. Therefore,\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
