    "q-314687": {
      "dom_id": "step-q314687",
      "name": "Question 17",
      "problem": "Using the definition of the hyperbolic cosine, calculate ${f}^{-1}(x)$ given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x-3)$ and $x\\ge \\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)+$\n{{field-2}}",
      "worked_solution": "Let $y=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x-3).$ Since $x\\ge \\frac{3}{2},$ we have $y\\ge 1.$\n\nTo find the inverse function, we swap $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2y-3),\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge \\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nNext, we apply the definition of hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,({e}^{2y-3}+{e}^{-(2y-3)}) \\\\ 2x & ={e}^{2y-3}+{e}^{-(2y-3)} \\\\ 2x{e}^{2y-3} & ={e}^{4y-6}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBringing everything to the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4y-6}-2x{e}^{2y-3}+1 & =0 \\\\ ({e}^{2y-3})^{2}-2x({e}^{2y-3})+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y={e}^{2y-3}.$ We can form a quadratic equation in $Y$ and solve it by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Y}^{2}-2xY+1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2}-{x}^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ Y-x & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ Y & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ {e}^{2y-3} & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ 2y-3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that for  $x\\ge 1,$ we have $x-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}\\le 1$ and  $x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}\\ge 1.$ Since $2y-3\\ge 0,$ the logarithm in the above equality must be positive, so we reject the alternative with the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}) \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})+3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Therefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})+\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
