    "q-341520": {
      "dom_id": "step-q341520",
      "name": "Question 18",
      "problem": "Using the definition of the hyperbolic sine, calculate ${f}^{-1}(x)$ given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\sqrt{x})$ for $x\\ge 0.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=($\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $y=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡\\sqrt{x}.$ Note that $f(x)$ is a one-to-one function on its entire domain. We have $y\\in [0,\\infty )$ for $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\nTo find the inverse function we swap $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡\\sqrt{y},\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $y\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\nNext, we apply the definition of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\sqrt{y}}-{e}^{-\\sqrt{y}}) \\\\ 2x & ={e}^{\\sqrt{y}}-{e}^{-\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ 2x{e}^{\\sqrt{y}} & ={e}^{2\\sqrt{y}}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBringing everything to the right-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2\\sqrt{y}}-2x{e}^{\\sqrt{y}}-1 & =0 \\\\ {({e}^{\\sqrt{y}})}^{2}-2x({e}^{\\sqrt{y}})-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y={e}^{\\sqrt{y}}.$ We can form a quadratic equation in $Y$ and solve it by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Y}^{2}-2xY-1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2}-{x}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2} & ={x}^{2}+1 \\\\ Y-x & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ {e}^{\\sqrt{y}} & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\ y & ={ln}^{2}⁡(x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we require real $y$ and $x<\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}$ for all $x,$ we select the positive solution. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}))}^{2}.\n$$",
