    "q-343418": {
      "dom_id": "step-q343418",
      "name": "Question 19",
      "problem": "Using the definition of the hyperbolic sine, calculate ${f}^{-1}(x)$ given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x-3).$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)+$\n{{field-2}}",
      "worked_solution": "Let $y=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x-3).$ Note that $f(x)$ is a one-to-one function on its entire domain, and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ for $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTo find the inverse function we swap $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2y-3),\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nNext, we apply the definition of hyperbolic sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{2y-3}-{e}^{-(2y-3)}) \\\\ 2x & ={e}^{2y-3}-{e}^{-(2y-3)} \\\\ 2x{e}^{2y-3} & ={e}^{2(2y-3)}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBringing everything to the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(2y-3)}-2x{e}^{2y-3}-1 & =0 \\\\ {({e}^{2y-3})}^{2}-2x({e}^{2y-3})-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y={e}^{2y-3}.$ We can form a quadratic equation in $Y$ and solve it by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Y}^{2}-2xY-1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2}-{x}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2} & ={x}^{2}+1 \\\\ Y-x & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ {e}^{2y-3} & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we require real $y,$ we must have ${e}^{2y-3}>0.$ However, $x-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}<0$ for all real $x.$ Therefore, we select the positive solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1})+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1})+\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$",
