    "q-341584": {
      "dom_id": "step-q341584",
      "name": "Question 20",
      "problem": "Using the definition of the hyperbolic cosine, calculate ${f}^{-1}(x)$ given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡((x-2)^{2})$ and $x\\ge 2.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Let $y=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡((x-2)^{2}).$ Since $x\\ge 2,$ we have $y\\ge 1.$\n\nTo find the inverse function, we swap $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡((y-2)^{2}),\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 2.$\n\nNext, we apply the definition of hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,({e}^{(y-2)^{2}}+{e}^{-(y-2)^{2}}) \\\\ 2x & ={e}^{(y-2)^{2}}+{e}^{-(y-2)^{2}} \\\\ 2x{e}^{(y-2)^{2}} & ={e}^{2(y-2)^{2}}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBringing everything to the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(y-2)^{2}}-2x{e}^{(y-2)^{2}}+1 & =0 \\\\ ({e}^{(y-2)^{2}})^{2}-2x({e}^{(y-2)^{2}})+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y={e}^{(y-2)^{2}}.$ We can form a quadratic equation in $Y$ and solve it by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Y}^{2}-2xY+1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2}-{x}^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ Y-x & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ Y & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ {e}^{(y-2)^{2}} & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that for  $x\\ge 1,$ we have $x-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}\\le 1$ and  $x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}\\ge 1.$ Since the logarithm in the above equality must be positive, we reject the alternative with the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}) \\\\ y-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})} \\\\ y & =2\\pm \\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want $y\\ge 2,$ select the positive solution. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})}+2.\n$$",
